## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of crash-consistency semantics, cross-socket memory ordering on x86-64 systems, and practical persistent memory programming patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Persistent Memory Semantics**: Understanding cache line flushes (clwb/clflushopt) and memory ordering fences (sfence/mfence)
- **Crash-Consistency Guarantees**: Ensuring the queue survives power loss without data corruption
- **Sequence Counter Design**: Handling wrap-around and enforcing total ordering across concurrent producers
- **Recovery Mechanisms**: Log-based recovery and ring buffer reconstruction after failure
- **Correctness Under Contention**: Two producers racing to reserve slots requires careful synchronization

## Code Example
```c
// Enqueue: reserve tail slot atomically
uint64_t tail = atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1);
Slot* slot = &q->slots[tail % N];

// Spin until slot is available
while (atomic_load(&slot->seq) != tail) { cpu_pause(); }

// Write payload to PMEM
memcpy(slot->payload, data, len);

// Persist in order: payload then sequence
atomic_store(&slot->seq, tail + 1);
clflushopt(slot);      // flush cache line to PMEM
sfence();               // ensure durability
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

